WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.220
So you can see on the front of this instrument
is a dome shaped device. That dome is a special

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.280
vessel which is made so we can control the
atmosphere inside and it is fabricated from

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.280
beryllium metal.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.340
This is the beryllium which I don’t really
want to get out.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.630
Why not?

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.250
Well, because it is a toxic chemical, a toxic
metal.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:27.930
Beryllium is an interesting element because
most chemists don’t really know about it

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.660
very much at all.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.410
So beryllium is a fantastic metal, it is really,
really interesting, it’s got really lots

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:39.129
of strange metallic properties, but one of
the most important to me is that it is transparent

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:43.329
to x-rays. So I can shine x-rays straight
through the beryllium and the beryllium does

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:47.440
not absorb it at all which most other metals
especially those with higher molecular weights

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.440
do.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.129
Beryllium-laden dust has been shown to cause
berylliosis which is actually an industrial

00:00:53.129 --> 00:01:00.699
lung disease which causes the formation of
swollen nodules in the lungs called granulomas

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:03.510
which is a bit gross, so I don’t really
want to open up the jar.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:10.610
It’s near the beginning of the periodic
table, but we rarely come across it.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:17.030
So at the moment we are at Matt’s lab and
we are doing some x-ray experiments on a really,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:22.790
really quite sensitive compound. So X-rays
themselves are absorbed by everything, ok,

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.520
so when we shoot it through the air it is
absorbed by the air, so to measure the x-ray

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.009
absorption of our compound which is sensitive
to the air we need to be able to remove the

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:37.720
air and encase it so we use beryllium to make
a magic box around our sample, that the x-rays

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.000
can come into, interact with our sample, and
then exit without interacting with the material

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.009
around them. So you can see on the front of
this instrument, there is a dome shaped device,

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:53.750
that dome, is a special vessel which is made
so that we could control the atmosphere inside

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.119
and it is fabricated from beryllium metal.

00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:01.369
I talk about in my lectures, I explain that
beryllium chloride the molecule that has three

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:06.450
atoms, two chlorine atoms and one beryllium
atom, has the three atoms arranged in the

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:12.370
row, so it is a straight molecule so called
linear molecule. I have never seen beryllium

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.690
chloride.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:19.500
Chemists use it on x-ray defractometers because
actually it is so lightweight it is actually

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.060
got atomic number 4.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:27.019
The beryllium allows the x-rays to come in
and out without interacting at all. It is

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:30.930
a fantastic device it is really useful for
really sensitive chemistry, it is a really

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.530
beautiful application. The cost of that beryllium
though, that is significant, that small beryllium

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.150
sample cost 15,000 Euros.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:49.920
It only has four protons and a number of neutrons
so if x-rays fall on atoms of beryllium they

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:57.459
are not scattered very much so it’s like
the difference between shining light through

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:03.220
a glass window and if you made it out of a
heavy material it would be like, shining through

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.510
paper, it would be scattered you wouldn’t
get a good image.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.590
I’m hoping to actually do some chemistry
with beryllium which is why I’ve bought

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:13.200
it. It is pretty toxic so we are going to
have to be very, very careful. You can’t

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.060
actually breathe in the dust at all, cause
even actually one exposure has shown with

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.560
some people one exposure is enough to cause
this sort of sensitive reaction.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:27.370
But beryllium is very poisonous so if you
do use these beryllium windows, you have to

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.230
be careful not to lick your fingers. Well
good chemists shouldn’t lick their fingers

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:31.769
anyway.

